<<~ ahu #/verse-binding>>

!! The Verse Marriage — witnessed bindings

Field-grounded against Epic's UEFN+/Scene Graph direction (2026-06-12, two research spirits):

| Verse / Scene Graph | has-stack |
|---|---|
| entity (empty container) | nameless carrier |
| component (sole semantics carrier) | component definition meme |
| default transform component | `loci` — the mandatory address |
| type-as-predicate (succeeds/fails on a value) | tag filter / rating-by-schema-compliance |
| effect specifiers as negative promises | grants bounding what a carrier never does |
| non-enumerable `weak_map` persistence | capability-keyed access — authority = possession |
| module-scoped short names / qualified paths | relative tags / qualified `lar:` URIs |
| prefab + sparse instance-override | skeleton meme + sparse CRDT patch |
| hierarchy = lifetime, never taxonomy | child carriers (`/SKILL`, `/meta`) = lifetime; taxonomy lives only in holdings |

''Grants refused at the altar:'' `<transacts>` rollback (assumes one speculative world; CRDT merges many) · per-player save-var persistence (weaker than `lar:` + CRDT; we export, never import) · the single-parent spatial tree (our graph runs multi-parent; lifetime edges alone propagate).

''The nominal divergence, named so it never drifts:'' Verse defines components nominally (`class(component)`); here a component's nominal anchor = its ''address'', minted open, judged by the rating ladder.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/runtime-twin>>

!! The runtime twin ~ a causal island runs as a nameless cap stack

The has-stack law has a ''runtime mirror''. Where a DATA entity is a nameless carrier whose `tags` are a stack of component memes, a RUNTIME entity — a sovereign ''causal island'' worker — is a nameless behavior whose ''cap stack'' is its identity. No carrier //${copula}-a// "wiki worker" or "telemetry worker"; an island //has// caps, and the caps speak for themselves. `composeIsland([...caps])` (`tw5/island-caps.ts`) folds a `#has` cap stack into the one `IslandBehavior` the sovereign kernel drives.

<<~ scale plane "data/tags→component-memes -> runtime/caps→IslandBehavior">>

The same grammar runs both planes: stack order carries precedence (a cap claims a signal before later caps; teardown mirrors LIFO), an absent/unresolved cap reads fertile not broken, and each cap injects its substrate shore (`role = capability ≠ platform`) so the same stack composes on node, browser, any vessel. The TW5 VM workers stand as cap stacks — wiki `[hasDispatch, hasProjection, …]`, admin `[hasEngineWatch, hasDispatch]`, telemetry `[hasCapture]` — each a nameless causal island.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/edges>>

